WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.409
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.137 --> 00:00:07.970
PAUL YOUNG: So yeah.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:09.370
Paul Young, as introduced.

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:11.280
For those of you who
haven't yet met me,

00:00:11.280 --> 00:00:14.490
job title is Go-To-Market
Director for SAP Google Cloud

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:15.450
History.

00:00:15.450 --> 00:00:19.130
Quickly is-- actually,
for some reason LinkedIn

00:00:19.130 --> 00:00:21.930
has decided to auto generate
a post this week that says,

00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:25.020
congratulate Paul on spending
one year at Google Cloud.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:26.880
And so currently my
email has been bombarded

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.170
by people congratulating me.

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:32.610
So I guess I've been a year in
three days in role at Google.

00:00:32.610 --> 00:00:36.530
Before that, 20 years at FCPA,
eight years at Hewlett-Packard

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:39.790
deploying, running their
FCP infrastructure.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.480
So if you do math,
that means I'm old.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:44.610
Right?

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:50.967
And we've come a long way in one
year, I'll tell you, at Google.

00:00:50.967 --> 00:00:53.550
So just to give you an example
really quickly of where we are.

00:00:56.310 --> 00:01:00.330
June last year at
Next San Francisco,

00:01:00.330 --> 00:01:03.720
we announced that we'd have
the 12 and 18-terabytes VMs

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:04.800
available.

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:07.980
And it takes an act of God to
preannounce anything at Google,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.962
I have to tell you.

00:01:09.962 --> 00:01:11.670
In order to get that
to work, we actually

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.170
had to buy the hardware,
install it, and make

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.600
sure it ran, and would do
everything we claimed it would

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:17.308
before we were allowed
to preannounce it.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:24.300
In our world it was
a pretty fixed event

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.130
that we'd committed
to launching these,

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:27.360
and we were going to launch
them, because we already

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:28.410
had the hardware.

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.500
That said, as obviously,
Brad announced this morning

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:36.590
in the keynote, the 12 and
the 6 are available right now

00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.580
in early access to
customers, to become

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:39.840
fully available this half.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:42.180
And so politely I'll
say promises made

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.323
and promises kept.

00:01:43.323 --> 00:01:44.865
And at the time,
I'll tell you, there

00:01:44.865 --> 00:01:48.680
was a lot of questioning
on whether we were going

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:51.840
to do this, because no one
had produced a VM bigger

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.048
than 4 terabytes at the time.

00:01:53.048 --> 00:01:55.590
There was a question whether it
was even technically possible

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:57.090
and what it would do.

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.180
But we've delivered it.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:02:03.352
And we've delivered on a plan
here with great intention,

00:02:03.352 --> 00:02:04.560
and a plan that continues on.

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:06.435
And I wanted to just
fill in some gaps really

00:02:06.435 --> 00:02:08.430
quickly here to explain why.

00:02:08.430 --> 00:02:13.410
We've been obsessed with being
the only cloud provider that's

00:02:13.410 --> 00:02:15.930
doing full-on VM for
our entire stack,

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:19.290
because we believe it opens up
huge potential for how you run

00:02:19.290 --> 00:02:22.230
SAP and how you
can run that better

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:24.660
and how you can manage
the change that comes day

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:28.470
to day with running
SAP environments.

00:02:28.470 --> 00:02:30.473
Just to give you
one quick example.

00:02:30.473 --> 00:02:32.890
Brad talked about this earlier
as well in the environment.

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:36.180
We had a customer in the life
sciences space who came to us

00:02:36.180 --> 00:02:37.140
and said, look.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.570
One of our big
challenges is downtime.

00:02:39.570 --> 00:02:40.350
Right?

00:02:40.350 --> 00:02:42.840
The law being what the law
is right now that if you're

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:45.340
in the pharmaceutical business,
you're in the medical device

00:02:45.340 --> 00:02:47.700
business, you fundamentally
can't manufacture

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:49.380
if SAP isn't online.

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:50.310
Right?

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:52.200
You're required to record
for pharmaceuticals

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.575
the ingredients that were
used, the recipe that was used.

00:02:54.575 --> 00:02:56.492
And if it's not online,
you can't record that.

00:02:56.492 --> 00:02:57.930
If you're building
pacemakers, you

00:02:57.930 --> 00:02:59.550
have to record the battery
and who it came from

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:02.092
and what batch it came from, so
that in the event of a fault,

00:03:02.092 --> 00:03:04.560
we can trace everybody who has
a pacemaker with that batch

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.300
of batteries installed in it.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.060
And so literally, if
SAP isn't running,

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:12.570
then they have to shut
down the entire company

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:13.840
until it comes back up again.

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.630
So the CIO basically said, look.

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:17.820
We're a $10 billion
a year manufacturer

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.350
in the life sciences space.

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:23.780
And every hour where SAP is down
it cost us a million dollars.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:26.810
And the answer was, if you
can do something about that,

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.170
then that's something
better, right?

00:03:29.170 --> 00:03:30.670
And again, coming
back to why are we

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:32.902
VM-based, why do
we care about VMs?

00:03:32.902 --> 00:03:34.360
We're VM-based and
we care about it

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:35.818
that much because
one of the things

00:03:35.818 --> 00:03:37.030
we have is live migration.

00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:38.050
Right?

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.260
So what happens is basically
that if we detect a patch needs

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.270
to be put in for
security purposes,

00:03:43.270 --> 00:03:45.490
instead of what everybody
else on the planet does,

00:03:45.490 --> 00:03:46.907
which is send you
an email saying,

00:03:46.907 --> 00:03:49.027
you have 72 hours to
shut down your systems

00:03:49.027 --> 00:03:50.860
and take a three-hour
outage while you patch

00:03:50.860 --> 00:03:54.048
your environment, which is
$3 million in lost revenue

00:03:54.048 --> 00:03:55.090
you weren't planning for.

00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:58.660
What happens in our
world is, a brand new VM

00:03:58.660 --> 00:04:00.642
powers up at exactly
the same size and shape

00:04:00.642 --> 00:04:01.600
of what you're running.

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:03.340
It's pre-patched for security.

00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:05.980
And then we replicate the
system across while it

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:08.350
runs-- the memory, the
CPU state, the disk.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:10.560
And when we achieve sync,
your original drops out

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:11.770
and, you continue onwards.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:15.980
And $3 million saved every time
we do that for one customer.

00:04:15.980 --> 00:04:17.230
Right?

00:04:17.230 --> 00:04:21.750
And up until now
nobody can do that.

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:24.033
Right now only we can do that.

00:04:24.033 --> 00:04:26.200
Only we can do it for
anything over half a terabyte.

00:04:26.200 --> 00:04:27.575
We're going all
the way up to 12.

00:04:27.575 --> 00:04:28.570
And that graph's at 18.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:29.920
Right?

00:04:29.920 --> 00:04:32.480
The bigger you get, the
bigger the number is.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.510
And so that's, I think,
the bit you first

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:36.100
want to understand about where
we're at in Google Cloud,

00:04:36.100 --> 00:04:36.600
right?

00:04:36.600 --> 00:04:38.800
Is we didn't enter
this market saying--

00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.440
uncannily, when I came on
board, the conversation

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:44.410
was, hey, if we just want to
be a hosting provider for SAP,

00:04:44.410 --> 00:04:46.570
then let's just go
buy a hosting company.

00:04:46.570 --> 00:04:47.200
Right?

00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:48.825
We don't want to be
a hosting provider.

00:04:48.825 --> 00:04:51.913
We want to be the best
run environment for SAP.

00:04:51.913 --> 00:04:53.330
And that's the
first step of that.

00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:55.798
I'll give you a separate
example of where we're at.

00:04:55.798 --> 00:04:58.090
One of the other things,
again, what everybody else has

00:04:58.090 --> 00:05:00.910
to do right now is you have
to take a three-year commit

00:05:00.910 --> 00:05:02.500
to an SAP server.

00:05:02.500 --> 00:05:07.630
So you come through and you say,
hey, I have my plan for SAP.

00:05:07.630 --> 00:05:10.210
From the beginning of
time, what SAP says

00:05:10.210 --> 00:05:12.010
is we need to go
do a quick sizer.

00:05:12.010 --> 00:05:14.800
And you need to tell me what
your busiest hour is going

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.785
to be three years from now.

00:05:17.785 --> 00:05:19.660
And we're going to buy
all of that equipment,

00:05:19.660 --> 00:05:20.743
and we're going to run it.

00:05:20.743 --> 00:05:22.508
And hopefully you've
sized it correctly.

00:05:22.508 --> 00:05:24.550
But you're going to run
all that equipment hoping

00:05:24.550 --> 00:05:26.710
you get your growth rates right,
hoping you get your customer

00:05:26.710 --> 00:05:27.850
demand right, and
everything else to where

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.590
you need to be in three years.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:32.350
So I've done this conversation
a lot with the retailers.

00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:35.080
My first question generally I
say in the retail conversations

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.780
is, does anyone know--

00:05:37.780 --> 00:05:39.280
there's a risk here,
because I think

00:05:39.280 --> 00:05:40.750
there is someone in the
audience who knows this.

00:05:40.750 --> 00:05:41.810
So don't answer it.

00:05:41.810 --> 00:05:44.620
But does anyone know
what the best-selling toy

00:05:44.620 --> 00:05:46.390
was three years ago?

00:05:46.390 --> 00:05:49.270
So Christmas 2015.

00:05:49.270 --> 00:05:52.200
Anyone know what the
number one selling toy was?

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:52.700
No.

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:53.205
No.

00:05:53.205 --> 00:05:54.080
I've done this a lot.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:55.635
Generally people don't know.

00:05:55.635 --> 00:05:57.260
You can check me on
this, but I believe

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.540
the number one selling
toy Christmas 2015

00:05:59.540 --> 00:06:01.280
was an Elsa doll.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.230
And the fact that none
of us have remembered

00:06:03.230 --> 00:06:07.640
that is indicative of how little
we know about history, never

00:06:07.640 --> 00:06:08.490
mind the future.

00:06:08.490 --> 00:06:08.990
Right?

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:13.460
If anyone can tell me what the
top selling toy is in 2022,

00:06:13.460 --> 00:06:15.740
then we're both in
the wrong business.

00:06:15.740 --> 00:06:16.372
Right?

00:06:16.372 --> 00:06:18.830
And given that you can't do
that, the concept that you know

00:06:18.830 --> 00:06:21.870
how big your SAP system needs
to be three years from now,

00:06:21.870 --> 00:06:25.830
and you can contract with me
for that capacity, is ludicrous.

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:26.330
Right?

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.370
But it's what
everybody else does.

00:06:28.370 --> 00:06:29.960
Except us.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:31.800
Because we're virtual
machine-based.

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:35.070
So what you don't buy
from me is fixed machines.

00:06:35.070 --> 00:06:37.270
What you buy from
me is capacity.

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:37.912
Right?

00:06:37.912 --> 00:06:40.370
And if you need more than that
capacity two years from now,

00:06:40.370 --> 00:06:40.870
it's OK.

00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:43.420
You can just increase the
capacity that you need.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:43.970
Right?

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:45.150
Likewise, what you're
looking at here

00:06:45.150 --> 00:06:46.567
is a command where
we're basically

00:06:46.567 --> 00:06:50.037
driving 15 terabytes of memory
and committing it in Frankfurt.

00:06:50.037 --> 00:06:51.370
Let's say you need 12 terabytes.

00:06:51.370 --> 00:06:52.880
But you don't need
12 terabytes now.

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:53.540
Right?

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:55.420
Right now you've got a bunch of
little dev boxes and everything

00:06:55.420 --> 00:06:55.920
else.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.190
So what you need right
now is 24 half terabytes.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:06:58.850
Great.

00:06:58.850 --> 00:07:00.890
Let's say tomorrow you
need 6 one terabyte

00:07:00.890 --> 00:07:01.760
and 3 two terabytes.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:02.605
Great.

00:07:02.605 --> 00:07:04.730
Let's say the day after
you need 1 twelve terabyte.

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.570
Great.

00:07:06.570 --> 00:07:08.070
You just restructure
as you need to.

00:07:08.070 --> 00:07:10.230
No one's done that
flexibility before.

00:07:10.230 --> 00:07:11.200
Right?

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:13.700
So there's my very quick state
of the union on where we are.

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:14.990
We made a lot of
promises last year

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:17.323
to customers where we said,
we're going to be different.

00:07:17.323 --> 00:07:19.340
We're going to have the
best run environments.

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:20.680
Politely, we've delivered.

00:07:20.680 --> 00:07:22.440
We've delivered on time.

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:27.120
And secondarily politely,
we've only just started.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:28.970
There's a lot more to come.

00:07:28.970 --> 00:07:31.150
But with that, I've
always said in this space

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:35.640
that SAP customers don't
actually buy from salespeople.

00:07:35.640 --> 00:07:38.240
SAP customers buy
from SAP customers.

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:40.580
Generally it's a very
small but very active

00:07:40.580 --> 00:07:43.220
tribe where when one does
one, and it's successful,

00:07:43.220 --> 00:07:46.760
everybody else will learn
about it and will follow.

00:07:46.760 --> 00:07:51.790
So objective for the year was,
first of all, land customers.

00:07:51.790 --> 00:07:56.360
Secondary and politely, land
customers of significance.

00:07:56.360 --> 00:07:57.290
Right?

00:07:57.290 --> 00:07:59.150
So land the biggest
customers we can handle,

00:07:59.150 --> 00:08:01.247
because the challenge
is you start small.

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:03.080
And the average SAP
customer will come along

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:03.660
and say, well, yeah.

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:05.420
They're in my industry, and
yeah, they're in my country.

00:08:05.420 --> 00:08:06.350
But I'm bigger than them.

00:08:06.350 --> 00:08:07.183
Can you really work?

00:08:07.183 --> 00:08:09.150
There's always going
to be skepticism.

00:08:09.150 --> 00:08:11.972
So the answer was, who are
the biggest customers--

00:08:11.972 --> 00:08:14.180
in our dreams, who are the
biggest customers we could

00:08:14.180 --> 00:08:16.550
possibly land that would
prove to this business

00:08:16.550 --> 00:08:18.920
that we're serious, we're
here, we do the enterprise,

00:08:18.920 --> 00:08:21.750
and we can do SAP at the
largest scale possible?

00:08:21.750 --> 00:08:24.710
And one of my dream customers
on that list was McKesson.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:27.260
And through good luck
and good fortune,

00:08:27.260 --> 00:08:30.680
McKesson is a customer for SAP
for Google Cloud at this point.

00:08:30.680 --> 00:08:32.730
So thank you for the business.

00:08:32.730 --> 00:08:35.419
But once again, I could tell
you about this all afternoon.

00:08:35.419 --> 00:08:36.950
It doesn't really matter.

00:08:36.950 --> 00:08:38.523
Far more important
and far better

00:08:38.523 --> 00:08:40.440
is for you to hear from
a customer themselves.

00:08:40.440 --> 00:08:44.200
So I'd like to welcome on stage,
Meena Kishanchandani and Andy

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.107
Zitney from McKesson

00:08:46.107 --> 00:08:49.029
[APPLAUSE]

00:08:52.087 --> 00:08:53.420
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Hey, Paul.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.607
That was almost a perfect
pronunciation of my last name.

00:08:56.607 --> 00:08:58.440
PAUL YOUNG: I've been
dreading-- lately I've

00:08:58.440 --> 00:08:59.732
been dreading that one all day.

00:08:59.732 --> 00:09:01.040
I have to tell you.

00:09:01.040 --> 00:09:04.150
I've was round the
back rehearsing.

00:09:04.150 --> 00:09:06.420
You can correct me for--
your coming back tomorrow

00:09:06.420 --> 00:09:07.290
for another event.

00:09:07.290 --> 00:09:08.130
So.

00:09:08.130 --> 00:09:08.400
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: But no.

00:09:08.400 --> 00:09:09.400
That was almost perfect.

00:09:09.400 --> 00:09:11.483
PAUL YOUNG: I get points
for that one, apparently.

00:09:11.483 --> 00:09:12.268
So thanks.

00:09:12.268 --> 00:09:14.310
So both of you, A, thanks
for giving us the time.

00:09:14.310 --> 00:09:16.728
Thanks for your
business, as always.

00:09:16.728 --> 00:09:18.270
Generally, these
things, the tendency

00:09:18.270 --> 00:09:20.200
is to want to talk
about technology.

00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:21.070
Right?

00:09:21.070 --> 00:09:22.445
We're at a technology
conference.

00:09:22.445 --> 00:09:25.710
Frankly, in this space I believe
this is as much about people

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:26.793
as it is about technology.

00:09:26.793 --> 00:09:28.543
So let's make this a
little more personal.

00:09:28.543 --> 00:09:29.210
Do you want to--

00:09:29.210 --> 00:09:31.320
can I give the audience
a little background

00:09:31.320 --> 00:09:32.902
on your career path so far?

00:09:32.902 --> 00:09:34.860
What gets you to your
current role at McKesson?

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:35.880
Who are you?

00:09:35.880 --> 00:09:38.100
ANDY ZITNEY: Will I start?

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:39.000
I'll start.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.250
So I'm Andy Zitney,
CPO from McKesson.

00:09:42.250 --> 00:09:45.950
Background, a lot of financial
industry, including PayPal.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:49.400
I see the PayPal contingent.

00:09:49.400 --> 00:09:52.790
So a lot of stuff learned
from all of those episodes

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:56.420
with PayPal, JP
Morgan, Allstate.

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:59.048
I think Allstate was an
almost 70-year-old company.

00:09:59.048 --> 00:10:00.590
And I thought that
wasn't old enough.

00:10:00.590 --> 00:10:04.200
So I left there and joined
a 180-year-old company

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:06.650
in the health care
industry, and always

00:10:06.650 --> 00:10:08.060
sort of ran toward the fire.

00:10:08.060 --> 00:10:09.870
And I think in the
health care industry

