to the room and
getting the engineers

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:07.722
sitting together and talking.

00:33:07.722 --> 00:33:09.680
Nothing against all the
sales team that's here.

00:33:09.680 --> 00:33:12.560
But getting the engineers in
the room and working together,

00:33:12.560 --> 00:33:14.000
they got excited themselves.

00:33:14.000 --> 00:33:16.340
And once she could
see them start

00:33:16.340 --> 00:33:20.600
to think on the whiteboards
and start walking through this,

00:33:20.600 --> 00:33:22.290
that became the art
of the possible.

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:25.610
And then layer on there a lot
of the automation conversations

00:33:25.610 --> 00:33:26.940
that came into play.

00:33:26.940 --> 00:33:27.440
Right?

00:33:27.440 --> 00:33:29.600
I think that was a big
differentiator when

00:33:29.600 --> 00:33:32.390
we were taking a look
at the partner, right?

00:33:32.390 --> 00:33:35.790
Google comes in with a software
engineering mindset first.

00:33:35.790 --> 00:33:37.732
And not a lot of the
others out there did.

00:33:37.732 --> 00:33:38.440
PAUL YOUNG: Yeah.

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:41.210
Very cool.

00:33:41.210 --> 00:33:44.740
One of my favorite questions and
Andy's least favorite question.

00:33:44.740 --> 00:33:48.510
Two, three, five years from
now, how do we measure success?

00:33:48.510 --> 00:33:50.150
How do we know we've
been successful?

00:33:50.150 --> 00:33:51.780
You're gonna say, Meena, can
you answer that question?

00:33:51.818 --> 00:33:51.856
ANDY ZITNEY: Yeah.

00:33:51.856 --> 00:33:52.523
I was gonna say.

00:33:52.523 --> 00:33:53.330
Meena goes first.

00:33:53.330 --> 00:33:56.240
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
So I think every year

00:33:56.240 --> 00:34:00.120
that success criteria
is going to change.

00:34:00.120 --> 00:34:04.140
So I'm only thinking one
year at a time at this time.

00:34:04.140 --> 00:34:12.270
So for me, over the next year,
it's quantitative, qualitative.

00:34:12.270 --> 00:34:16.550
So if we can get our SAP
Europe implementation,

00:34:16.550 --> 00:34:22.699
S4 implementation, the
production deployed

00:34:22.699 --> 00:34:28.040
and working, and where
there's great user experience,

00:34:28.040 --> 00:34:30.770
both from IT and
our business users.

00:34:30.770 --> 00:34:32.139
I think that's a huge--

00:34:32.139 --> 00:34:35.489
that's a number
one, in my opinion,

00:34:35.489 --> 00:34:38.900
because that is our
opportunity to really sell--

00:34:38.900 --> 00:34:42.260
you know, basically
convince everybody else

00:34:42.260 --> 00:34:44.489
that this is going to work.

00:34:44.489 --> 00:34:48.650
I think the other thing
is, if you can drastically

00:34:48.650 --> 00:34:53.040
reduce our reliance on
our external partners,

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:55.730
including Google, where
our people can do things

00:34:55.730 --> 00:34:59.980
on their own, because they're
really kind of ramped up.

00:34:59.980 --> 00:35:01.940
I think that's
another one for me.

00:35:01.940 --> 00:35:06.240
And I think finally, the third
one is that for North America,

00:35:06.240 --> 00:35:08.690
if it can move the
dial forward and get

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:13.580
some of our SAP cloud-ready
type environments

00:35:13.580 --> 00:35:15.170
successfully migrated.

00:35:15.170 --> 00:35:16.970
I think for me, that's like--

00:35:16.970 --> 00:35:18.500
I'm just looking
one year at a time,

00:35:18.500 --> 00:35:23.383
because I don't even want to
think about it beyond that.

00:35:23.383 --> 00:35:24.800
ANDY ZITNEY: I'll
start with where

00:35:24.800 --> 00:35:26.810
I started last
night, that I didn't

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:28.090
get fired because of this.

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:30.260
So that's where the bar sets.

00:35:30.260 --> 00:35:34.500
And then we go above that
and say a couple of things.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:37.290
I think the customer
experience has to change.

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:37.790
Right?

00:35:37.790 --> 00:35:40.680
This can't just be the
exact same environment.

00:35:40.680 --> 00:35:42.740
So it has to be an
environment that

00:35:42.740 --> 00:35:45.500
enables us to change
the business process,

00:35:45.500 --> 00:35:49.190
enable business process
change in a simplistic manner

00:35:49.190 --> 00:35:50.395
and add pace.

00:35:50.395 --> 00:35:51.770
And get the business
to the table

00:35:51.770 --> 00:35:55.010
thinking a lot different
than what we do today.

00:35:55.010 --> 00:35:57.860
And leveraging this environment
and the capabilities

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.350
within the environment
that gives the business

00:36:00.350 --> 00:36:03.740
an opportunity to think
differently on enhancements,

00:36:03.740 --> 00:36:06.860
as opposed to I go back to
that comment I made earlier,

00:36:06.860 --> 00:36:09.290
when you start thinking
about enhancement or change

00:36:09.290 --> 00:36:12.740
or customization, that's
a long process today

00:36:12.740 --> 00:36:14.352
in the current environment.

00:36:14.352 --> 00:36:16.310
It'll be nice when they're
sitting at the table

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:18.080
and it's an easy conversation.

00:36:18.080 --> 00:36:19.550
Says, you know, I
would really like

00:36:19.550 --> 00:36:23.180
to just change this and do
that in a different manner.

00:36:23.180 --> 00:36:24.080
Can we look at that?

00:36:24.080 --> 00:36:25.663
And we give them an
environment where,

00:36:25.663 --> 00:36:28.838
yeah, tell me that on a Monday,
and we can do it on a Friday.

00:36:28.838 --> 00:36:29.630
And they say, yeah.

00:36:29.630 --> 00:36:30.755
That's exactly what I want.

00:36:30.755 --> 00:36:32.480
And then we go live
to production, right?

00:36:32.480 --> 00:36:35.720
So it's enabling the business
to think much differently

00:36:35.720 --> 00:36:40.010
than what they do today in a
huge environment and a much

00:36:40.010 --> 00:36:42.860
hopefully much more
simplistic environment

00:36:42.860 --> 00:36:44.290
when we implement it.

00:36:44.290 --> 00:36:46.650
And then the last
thing I'd say is,

00:36:46.650 --> 00:36:50.200
Meena mentioned
keep the core hull.

00:36:50.200 --> 00:36:54.460
For us, that really means
all customizations-- well,

00:36:54.460 --> 00:36:57.370
a lot of the customizations,
90% of the customizations--

00:36:57.370 --> 00:36:59.830
are done on our custom
platform and not built

00:36:59.830 --> 00:37:03.250
into the core
purchased application.

00:37:03.250 --> 00:37:06.840
That gives us massive
change in the architecture.

00:37:06.840 --> 00:37:09.430
That would give us
upgradability, sustainability

00:37:09.430 --> 00:37:14.590
to the core, and much less
cost to maintain, and enable

00:37:14.590 --> 00:37:19.232
the business enhancements at
our pace with our resources.

00:37:19.232 --> 00:37:21.190
PAUL YOUNG: I think it's
interesting, actually,

00:37:21.190 --> 00:37:23.140
that the change thing
has started to come up

00:37:23.140 --> 00:37:25.723
with customers who've been with
us for a while, because again,

00:37:25.723 --> 00:37:29.020
in that VM-based
world, historically

00:37:29.020 --> 00:37:32.270
one of the things against
SAP is you can't change.

00:37:32.270 --> 00:37:32.770
Right.

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:35.890
And not that you can't
change, but you have an idea.

00:37:35.890 --> 00:37:38.330
But it's got to fit into the
change control mechanism.

00:37:38.330 --> 00:37:39.580
And so you want to justify it.

00:37:39.580 --> 00:37:40.455
And it takes a while.

00:37:40.455 --> 00:37:42.483
And you have to end
up to scale them up.

00:37:42.483 --> 00:37:44.650
And that ability to do small
incremental change that

00:37:44.650 --> 00:37:46.120
would make a huge
difference just

00:37:46.120 --> 00:37:48.115
isn't there in the platform.

00:37:48.115 --> 00:37:50.240
And a lot of that is driven
by hardware constraint.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:50.470
Right?

00:37:50.470 --> 00:37:52.553
We've see this a couple
customers have said, look,

00:37:52.553 --> 00:37:54.820
I can just copy off a QA system.

00:37:54.820 --> 00:37:56.360
I can try my concept.

00:37:56.360 --> 00:37:57.402
If I like it, it's good.

00:37:57.402 --> 00:37:59.110
I could approved it
and I can roll it in.

00:37:59.110 --> 00:38:01.035
And that ability
to kind of unlock

00:38:01.035 --> 00:38:03.160
the capacity of organization
to change and innovate

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:04.060
is fascinating.

00:38:04.060 --> 00:38:04.560
Right?

00:38:04.560 --> 00:38:06.587
It opens up a potential
in SAP that's not there.

00:38:06.587 --> 00:38:08.170
The other thing I'd
just add in really

00:38:08.170 --> 00:38:10.130
quickly is, on your
above the line,

00:38:10.130 --> 00:38:12.612
below the line
discussion, for some

00:38:12.612 --> 00:38:14.320
of the customers that
have been with us a

00:38:14.320 --> 00:38:17.930
while, there's one in particular
where I chased the CIO down

00:38:17.930 --> 00:38:20.710
a show recently, where I
said, I just want to check in

00:38:20.710 --> 00:38:21.850
that everything's going OK.

00:38:21.850 --> 00:38:22.030
Right?

00:38:22.030 --> 00:38:23.508
Because I haven't
heard from you.

00:38:23.508 --> 00:38:24.550
And his answer was, look.

00:38:24.550 --> 00:38:26.620
I've had-- I've been
live for nine months,

00:38:26.620 --> 00:38:28.328
and I've had zero
incidents where

00:38:28.328 --> 00:38:29.620
we've had to call Google Cloud.

00:38:29.620 --> 00:38:31.670
And he said, on the one
hand, it's fantastic.

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:33.087
On the other hand,
you know, I see

00:38:33.087 --> 00:38:37.030
a lot of my other salespeople,
because their system breaks.

00:38:37.030 --> 00:38:38.572
And I call them
and chat with them.

00:38:38.572 --> 00:38:40.030
And then we talk
about other stuff.

00:38:40.030 --> 00:38:40.570
Right?

00:38:40.570 --> 00:38:42.110
He's like, I never have to
call you to shout at you,

00:38:42.110 --> 00:38:43.340
so we never talk.

00:38:43.340 --> 00:38:45.430
And nice problem to have.

00:38:45.430 --> 00:38:48.192
However, it's
fascinating, because it's

00:38:48.192 --> 00:38:49.900
one of the things I
think long term we're

00:38:49.900 --> 00:38:50.950
going to have chase through.

00:38:50.950 --> 00:38:52.390
As much as the
hackathon and everything

00:38:52.390 --> 00:38:53.920
is really good where we're
having to invest and get

00:38:53.920 --> 00:38:55.000
your people to speak.

00:38:55.000 --> 00:38:56.692
I think ultimately
the conversation

00:38:56.692 --> 00:38:58.150
I've had with a
bunch of other CIOs

00:38:58.150 --> 00:39:02.840
is, ultimately they want us
to effectively be invisible.

00:39:02.840 --> 00:39:03.340
Right?

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:05.800
They want us to be a utility
that's just there and works

00:39:05.800 --> 00:39:07.200
and [INAUDIBLE] happens.

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:09.640
And so I think we're still
finding their way in terms

00:39:09.640 --> 00:39:12.340
of how do you engage
with the customer,

00:39:12.340 --> 00:39:14.450
contribute, make
them successful,

00:39:14.450 --> 00:39:16.540
but equally know
when to back off

00:39:16.540 --> 00:39:19.050
and then leave them with
us in the utility space.

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:21.550
Does that make sense in your
above the line, below the line?

00:39:21.550 --> 00:39:22.300
ANDY ZITNEY: Yeah.

00:39:22.300 --> 00:39:27.070
And it's-- the whole dynamic
above and below that value line

00:39:27.070 --> 00:39:30.880
is, that line was
flat in that drawing.

00:39:30.880 --> 00:39:32.090
It's definitely not flat.

00:39:32.090 --> 00:39:32.590
Right?

00:39:32.590 --> 00:39:34.715
So it goes across a bunch
of business capabilities.

00:39:34.715 --> 00:39:36.550
And depending on the
business capability

00:39:36.550 --> 00:39:39.380
is depending on where you want
to draw that line, in my mind.

00:39:39.380 --> 00:39:39.880
Right?

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:41.963
Because you could draw
that line very low and say,

00:39:41.963 --> 00:39:44.290
there is a lot above that
line that adds value,

00:39:44.290 --> 00:39:47.360
and it's a differentiator
in the business.

00:39:47.360 --> 00:39:50.110
So let's have more money
invested above that line.

00:39:50.110 --> 00:39:53.840
There's others that
for core services--

00:39:53.840 --> 00:39:56.770
and I think SAP core
services is one of those--

00:39:56.770 --> 00:39:59.030
there's a lot below that line.

00:39:59.030 --> 00:40:00.290
There's a ton below that line.

00:40:00.290 --> 00:40:04.900
And we would like to get that
to your point as a commodity,

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:06.790
and just deliver
it to the business.

00:40:06.790 --> 00:40:09.520
Then let them think of
things like pricing engines

00:40:09.520 --> 00:40:11.320
and everything else
above that line,

00:40:11.320 --> 00:40:14.623
and put all their focus up
there as opposed to us talking

00:40:14.623 --> 00:40:17.290
to them about we have an upgrade
coming, we have a patch coming,

00:40:17.290 --> 00:40:18.970
we're going to have
an outage for it.

00:40:18.970 --> 00:40:19.940
And everything else.

00:40:19.940 --> 00:40:21.955
They shouldn't have those
conversations anyway.

00:40:21.955 --> 00:40:23.080
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:40:23.080 --> 00:40:27.870
For example, to add an upgrade
today in one of the largest

00:40:27.870 --> 00:40:29.320
environment that I'm talking--

00:40:29.320 --> 00:40:35.560
I talked about, it takes
anywhere between 9 to 12 to 18

00:40:35.560 --> 00:40:39.070
months, and millions
and millions

00:40:39.070 --> 00:40:42.370
of dollars of investment
because of how complex

00:40:42.370 --> 00:40:47.500
it is and all of the stuff
that we've built on top of it

00:40:47.500 --> 00:40:48.485
over the years.

00:40:48.485 --> 00:40:50.360
PAUL YOUNG: Like, I said
earlier, stay tuned.

00:40:50.360 --> 00:40:53.470
But as you're aware, we've
got some other stuff.

00:40:53.470 --> 00:40:54.810
We're not done innovating yet.

00:40:54.810 --> 00:40:57.876
How about that for an answer?

00:40:57.876 --> 00:41:02.680
On that I shall say no
more before PR mugs me.

00:41:02.680 --> 00:41:06.570
For both of you, I would
say we're a year on.

00:41:06.570 --> 00:41:08.220
We're effectively
nine months on.

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:09.720
What do you know
now that you wish

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:14.130
you knew nine months ago when
you were starting this process?

00:41:14.130 --> 00:41:16.775
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI:
So I think I'm not

00:41:16.775 --> 00:41:17.900
sure about the nine months.

00:41:17.900 --> 00:41:21.780
But it's-- I'd probably go
back to the experience with--

00:41:21.780 --> 00:41:24.980
at least I've had,
or my team has had--

00:41:24.980 --> 00:41:27.950
on working with
the cloud on some--

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:30.120
even prior to those nine months.

00:41:30.120 --> 00:41:34.670
So our journey, I'm sure at
McKesson, has been on for 4

00:41:34.670 --> 00:41:35.840
some years?

00:41:35.840 --> 00:41:40.580
And some of the SAP folks
have been also trying

00:41:40.580 --> 00:41:43.970
to build some capabilities for
at least a couple of years.

00:41:43.970 --> 00:41:45.470
So I'll go back
to that reference

00:41:45.470 --> 00:41:48.980
point and not the nine
months reference point.

00:41:48.980 --> 00:41:54.260
So it has to be obviously,
it's a no-brainer that you have

00:41:54.260 --> 00:41:57.510
to kind of invest
in your people,

00:41:57.510 --> 00:41:59.690
because traditional ways
of thinking sometimes

00:41:59.690 --> 00:42:02.590
takes time to change.

00:42:02.590 --> 00:42:07.460
Then you have to really
kind of ramp up quickly

00:42:07.460 --> 00:42:08.700
to get some change going.

00:42:08.700 --> 00:42:14.420
I think you have to partner
with the external expertise

00:42:14.420 --> 00:42:18.230
and experience as well while
your staff is coming up.

00:42:18.230 --> 00:42:22.470
Talk to customers who
have done this before.

00:42:22.470 --> 00:42:24.420
And then from a
process perspective,

00:42:24.420 --> 00:42:27.680
I'd say really
invest in automation.

00:42:27.680 --> 00:42:33.510
Assess what your team is
spending the most time

00:42:33.510 --> 00:42:35.400
on doing manual tasks.

00:42:35.400 --> 00:42:39.060
Those are candidates for
automation from day one.

00:42:39.060 --> 00:42:41.120
So definitely that
should be your focus

00:42:41.120 --> 00:42:45.500
as you're thinking
about moving to cloud.

00:42:45.500 --> 00:42:50.480
The other thing that comes to
mind is challenge this 24 by 7

00:42:50.480 --> 00:42:54.860
assumptions for some of your
non-production environments,

00:42:54.860 --> 00:42:59.320
because it's not as--

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:00.800
ANDY ZITNEY: Tough for a period.

00:43:00.800 --> 00:43:02.800
And I think there's also
something [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:02.800 --> 00:43:05.300
to speed at the end of the
day, because it does clean up

00:43:05.300 --> 00:43:06.320
your environment,
and you don't have

00:43:06.320 --> 00:43:07.737
to worry about all
the integration

00:43:07.737 --> 00:43:09.510
points at the end of the day.

00:43:09.510 --> 00:43:10.760
PAUL YOUNG: It is fascinating.

00:43:10.760 --> 00:43:13.490
Because again, if you define it
as lift and shift, it's easy.

00:43:13.490 --> 00:43:15.532
It's just the stuff that's
there it's over there.

00:43:15.532 --> 00:43:17.600
It's just a SpongeBob
meeting, basically, right?

00:43:17.600 --> 00:43:19.100
The point where you
realize that you

00:43:19.100 --> 00:43:20.475
can get additional
value from it,

00:43:20.475 --> 00:43:22.030
it's a lot more thinking, right?

00:43:22.030 --> 00:43:22.780
ANDY ZITNEY: Yeah.

00:43:22.780 --> 00:43:24.780
PAUL YOUNG: And it's not a
technical problem anymore.

00:43:24.780 --> 00:43:25.600
It's not just a--

00:43:25.600 --> 00:43:27.267
hey, here's a tool,
and you can move it.

00:43:27.267 --> 00:43:28.680
There's a lot more--

00:43:28.680 --> 00:43:29.800
it's a project.

00:43:29.800 --> 00:43:30.320
Right?

00:43:30.320 --> 00:43:32.330
It involves business
people and changing

00:43:32.330 --> 00:43:35.480
people's lives and everyone
else has had much more work.

00:43:35.480 --> 00:43:40.190
Last question says, well
again, nine months ago we all--

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:43.030
a group of people
had lunch from here.

00:43:43.030 --> 00:43:44.030
That's where we started.

00:43:44.030 --> 00:43:46.260
Not where you guys started.

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:47.983
And now we're here.

00:43:47.983 --> 00:43:49.400
Equally, there's
a bunch of people

00:43:49.400 --> 00:43:51.410
sitting in this
audience who likely

00:43:51.410 --> 00:43:55.220
are here for the first time
having lunch and thinking

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:56.690
about getting started.

00:43:56.690 --> 00:43:59.550
What would you tell them?

00:43:59.550 --> 00:44:01.140
Other than enjoy lunch.

00:44:01.140 --> 00:44:04.150
ANDY ZITNEY: I would say, how
do you get started is just that.

00:44:04.150 --> 00:44:06.810
Go get started.

00:44:06.810 --> 00:44:09.420
I guess, Mike, I would
answer it with a question.

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:11.130
Well, what's plan B?

00:44:11.130 --> 00:44:13.060
What's your other options?

00:44:13.060 --> 00:44:17.290
Is it like for like and
maintaining the same?

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:20.920
And I think if
that's your answer,

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:22.590
you may want to rethink it.

00:44:22.590 --> 00:44:26.010
Because the business is going
to look at as more the same

00:44:26.010 --> 00:44:26.593
and slow down.

00:44:26.593 --> 00:44:28.593
And I think you'll have
a different conversation

00:44:28.593 --> 00:44:29.560
with your business.

00:44:29.560 --> 00:44:32.130
You go to them with
a strategy that

00:44:32.130 --> 00:44:36.510
is demonstrating not necessarily
that value line slide,

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:38.580
but demonstrating
that conversation

00:44:38.580 --> 00:44:41.880
that you really are trying to
free up the company's resources

00:44:41.880 --> 00:44:43.420
to plow into that.

00:44:43.420 --> 00:44:48.510
The differentiating business
processing above that line,

00:44:48.510 --> 00:44:51.580
and this is how we think we
can do it and articulate it.

00:44:51.580 --> 00:44:53.430
I think it's a much
better conversation,

00:44:53.430 --> 00:44:55.852
and they'll be more
eager to get started.

00:44:55.852 --> 00:44:56.655
PAUL YOUNG: Meena?

00:44:56.655 --> 00:44:57.780
MEENA KISHANCHANDANI: Yeah.

00:44:57.780 --> 00:45:01.500
I think-- I think
most of what Andy said

00:45:01.500 --> 00:45:07.800
is what I'm thinking as well,
because like for like is really

00:45:07.800 --> 00:45:10.040
there's no business case
there, to be honest.

00:45:10.040 --> 00:45:14.600
As least if you're like
McKesson or even close to it,

00:45:14.600 --> 00:45:18.390
it's just not going to work.

00:45:18.390 --> 00:45:24.390
So you have to be open to
kind of re-architecting your--

00:45:24.390 --> 00:45:26.380
not just from a
technology perspective,

00:45:26.380 --> 00:45:32.550
but also your kind
of not quite culture,

00:45:32.550 --> 00:45:35.700
I would say, but
how the messaging

00:45:35.700 --> 00:45:38.220
that goes from top
down to the people

00:45:38.220 --> 00:45:40.200
and how you energize
the base as well.

00:45:40.200 --> 00:45:43.590
Because that's, I think, one
of the most important things

00:45:43.590 --> 00:45:45.570
that if they're not
[INAUDIBLE],, then

00:45:45.570 --> 00:45:47.433
that's going to be
tough to achieve.

00:45:47.433 --> 00:45:49.600
PAUL YOUNG: Once again,
thank you for your business.

00:45:49.600 --> 00:45:50.310
Thank you for your time.

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:51.685
Sincerely, I really
do appreciate

00:45:51.685 --> 00:45:54.960
you taking time for sharing
your wisdom with everybody.

